WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.279
Environment variables are strings until the application validates and converts them.

2
00:00:04.279 --> 00:00:13.227
A value that is supposed to represent a boolean, integer, timeout, byte limit, path, URL, enumeration, or feature flag must be parsed carefully.

3
00:00:13.227 --> 00:00:20.618
The code should define accepted formats instead of accepting every spelling that happens to work in a quick test.

4
00:00:20.618 --> 00:00:24.897
Validation should cover range, units, allowed values, and relationships between settings.

5
00:00:24.897 --> 00:00:28.009
A timeout may need a minimum and maximum.

6
00:00:28.009 --> 00:00:31.900
A path may need to stay inside an allowed location.

7
00:00:31.900 --> 00:00:35.790
A debug setting may be incompatible with a production profile.

8
00:00:35.790 --> 00:00:39.680
A feature flag may require another setting to be present.

9
00:00:39.680 --> 00:00:44.348
These relationships should be checked before the value becomes trusted runtime state.

10
00:00:44.348 --> 00:00:46.682
Defaults should be conservative and documented.

11
00:00:46.682 --> 00:00:50.573
Missing or malformed values should not silently become unsafe behavior.

12
00:00:50.573 --> 00:00:53.685
Sometimes the safest response is to refuse startup.

13
00:00:53.685 --> 00:00:56.019
Sometimes a secure default is appropriate.

14
00:00:56.019 --> 00:01:03.021
Unknown values should also be handled deliberately because they may indicate typos, unsupported deployment profiles, or stale automation.

15
00:01:03.021 --> 00:01:08.856
After validation, application code should use typed trusted state instead of repeatedly interpreting raw strings.

16
00:01:08.856 --> 00:01:14.692
Clear validation errors help operators fix configuration problems, but diagnostics should avoid printing sensitive values.

17
00:01:14.692 --> 00:01:20.138
This boundary is where the environment stops being untrusted text and becomes deliberate configuration.

18
00:01:20.138 --> 00:01:22.472
Be especially careful with convenience parsing.

19
00:01:22.472 --> 00:01:31.809
Accepting every non-empty string as true, truncating large numbers, or treating parse failure as zero can turn a typo into an unsafe mode.

20
00:01:31.809 --> 00:01:39.200
Prefer explicit accepted spellings, checked numeric conversion, clear unit handling, and failure paths that do not relax security controls.
